  "worked_solution": "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+\u22ef+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot {x}^{3}+\u22ef+{x}^{7} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+35{x}^{3}+\u22ef\\,+{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}\u22481+7x+21{x}^{2}+35{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.04$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.96)^{7} & =(1+(-0.04))^{7} \\\\  & \u22481+7(-0.04)+21(-0.04)^{2}+35(-0.04)^{3} \\\\  & =1-7(0.04)+21(0.001\\,6)-35(0.000\\,064) \\\\  & =1-0.28+0.033\\,6-0.002\\,24 \\\\  & =0.751\\,36,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places.",
